flow "Write-through vs write-back caching"
summary "Write-through persists every write synchronously to storage for safety, while write-back replies immediately and risks losing unflushed data on crash."
tags caching durability storage
level intermediate
source "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cache_(computing)#Writing_policies"
related distributed-systems/cache-aside

node client "Client" actor
node cache "Cache" db tone=primary
node db "Database" db
edge client > cache
edge cache > db
array store "Value of x" keys=cache,db = v0 v0

scenario write-through "Write-through: synchronous durability"
  step write-req "Client writes x=v1 to cache"
    "The client sends `SET x=v1` to the cache layer. The cache updates its in-memory entry immediately."
    send client > cache "SET x=v1"
    label store#cache "v1"
    state cache active
  step sync-db "Cache writes synchronously to DB"
    "Under **write-through**, the cache waits to persist `x=v1` in the database before acknowledging the client. Write latency includes the DB round trip."
    send cache > db "WRITE x=v1"
    label store#db "v1"
    state db success
    send db > cache "persisted"
  step ack-safe "Reply OK: cache and DB match"
    "The client gets `OK` only after the DB confirms. Even if the cache crashes a millisecond later, `x=v1` is safely durable in the database."
    send cache > client "OK"
    state cache,store success
    callout db "Durable before client sees OK"

scenario crash-before-flush "Write-back: fast reply, lost on crash" from=write-through/write-req
  step fast-ack "Write-back replies OK before DB flush"
    "Under **write-back**, the cache marks `x=v1` as **dirty** and replies `OK` right away, planning to flush batches to the DB asynchronously for high write throughput."
    state store#cache warning
    label store#cache "v1 (dirty)"
    send cache > client "OK (fast!)"
    callout cache "Dirty entry not yet in DB"
  step crash "Cache crashes before background flush"
    "Power fails or the cache node restarts before the dirty entry flushes. The in-memory `v1` vanishes while the database still holds `v0`."
    state cache error
    effect cache shake
    label store#cache "LOST"
    state store#cache error with
  step data-loss "Acknowledged write is lost"
    "The client was told `OK`, yet `x=v1` is gone. Write-back trades durability for latency unless paired with battery-backed RAM or replicated logs."
    focus cache,db dim
    callout db "Still v0: acknowledged write lost"
